= Self-organized criticality =

'''Self-organized criticality''' (SOC) is a proposed class of behavior in driven, dissipative dynamical systems that evolve toward a critical-like state without precise external tuning of an ordinary control parameter. Near such a state, events can occur across many scales and statistical observables may show scale invariance, long-range correlations, or avalanche distributions without one characteristic event size.

Per Bak, Chao Tang, and Kurt Wiesenfeld introduced the concept in 1987 and developed it in 1988 as a possible explanation for scale-free structure and <math>1/f</math>-like noise in spatially extended systems.<ref name="btw1987">[https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.59.381 Bak, P., Tang, C., and Wiesenfeld, K. (1987), “Self-Organized Criticality: An Explanation of 1/f Noise,” ''Physical Review Letters'' 59, 381–384.]</ref><ref name="btw1988">[https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.38.364 Bak, P., Tang, C., and Wiesenfeld, K. (1988), “Self-organized criticality,” ''Physical Review A'' 38, 364–374.]</ref>

SOC is influential but should be applied carefully. Power laws, bursty events, or complex behavior by themselves do not establish a self-organized critical mechanism.

== Criticality ==

In equilibrium statistical physics, critical points occur at phase transitions where correlation lengths become large and systems can display scale invariance.

Classic examples require tuning a control parameter such as temperature toward a critical value.

SOC proposes a different route.

The internal dynamics of some slowly driven dissipative systems can move them toward a critical-like regime without an experimenter repeatedly adjusting an external parameter to the critical point.

== Driven dissipative systems ==

Canonical SOC models contain:

* external drive;
* local thresholds;
* relaxation or redistribution;
* dissipation;
* many interacting degrees of freedom.

The drive adds energy or material slowly.

Threshold crossings trigger cascades.

Dissipation prevents indefinite accumulation.

The separation between slow drive and fast relaxation is important in many models.

== Bak–Tang–Wiesenfeld sandpile ==

The best-known SOC model is a cellular-automaton sandpile.

Each lattice site holds an integer height.

Grains are added slowly.

When a site exceeds a threshold, it topples and redistributes grains to neighbors.

This can trigger neighboring sites to topple.

The cascade continues until all sites are stable.

Grains reaching open boundaries leave the system, providing dissipation.

== Avalanche ==

An '''avalanche''' is the chain of activity triggered by one drive event.

Avalanche quantities can include:

* size — number of topplings;
* area — number of distinct sites involved;
* duration;
* released energy.

SOC predicts broad event-size distributions over ranges that grow with system size.

The details depend on model and definition.

== Scale invariance ==

A scale-free distribution has no single characteristic event scale over the relevant range.

A simple candidate form is

<math>P(s)\propto s^{-\tau}.</math>

Finite systems cannot support an infinite pure power law.

Large events are cut off by finite system size.

Therefore realistic analysis uses finite-size scaling rather than fitting one line to a log-log plot.

== Finite-size scaling ==

A common form is

<math>P(s;L)\sim s^{-\tau}F(s/L^D),</math>

where:

* <math>L</math> is system size;
* <math>\tau</math> is an avalanche exponent;
* <math>D</math> controls the cutoff scale;
* <math>F</math> is a scaling function.

If data from different system sizes collapse after rescaling, the evidence for scaling is much stronger than a single apparent heavy tail.

== Power laws are not sufficient ==

Many mechanisms produce heavy-tailed distributions.

Possible alternatives include:

* lognormal processes;
* multiplicative growth;
* mixtures of exponentials;
* preferential attachment;
* thresholded noise.

Therefore:

<pre>
power law
≠ self-organized criticality
</pre>

A SOC claim needs evidence about the generating dynamics.

== Statistical fitting ==

Power-law fitting should specify:

* lower cutoff;
* exponent estimate;
* uncertainty;
* goodness of fit;
* comparison with alternatives.

Least-squares fitting on log-log axes can produce biased estimates.

Finite sample size can make a non-power-law distribution appear straight over a short range.

== Stationarity ==

SOC models often approach a statistically stationary driven state.

After transient behavior, average inflow is balanced by dissipation.

Measurements should exclude or separately analyze the transient.

A changing trend can create apparent long-range correlations that do not reflect criticality.

== Time-scale separation ==

The standard sandpile picture adds one grain only after the previous avalanche ends.

This separates:

* slow drive;
* fast relaxation.

Real systems can be driven continuously.

Avalanches can overlap.

When time-scale separation fails, the canonical SOC mechanism can change or disappear.

== Conservation and dissipation ==

Bulk redistribution in many sandpile models is locally conservative.

Dissipation occurs at boundaries.

This structure helps produce long-range cascades.

Nonconservative models can also show critical-like behavior, but the mechanism and universality class can differ.

Conservation should therefore be stated rather than assumed.

== Universality ==

Critical systems can share exponents even when microscopic details differ.

This is called universality.

SOC research has sought analogous universality classes.

However, not all driven threshold systems share one set of exponents.

Update rules, dimensionality, conservation, disorder, and stochasticity matter.

== Real sandpiles ==

The sandpile is an analogy as well as a model.

Real granular materials involve:

* friction;
* particle shape;
* inertia;
* rolling;
* packing.

They do not automatically behave like the BTW cellular automaton.

Frette and colleagues found SOC-like avalanche behavior in elongated rice grains but not in less elongated grains, showing that granular criticality depends on dissipation details rather than being universally insensitive to material properties.<ref name="rice">[https://doi.org/10.1038/379049a0 Frette, V. et al. (1996), “Avalanche dynamics in a pile of rice,” ''Nature'' 379, 49–52.]</ref>

== Breakdown in granular systems ==

Models can reproduce near-SOC behavior at small scale and large characteristic avalanches at larger scale.

Vieira's 2002 analysis explicitly examined breakdown of SOC-like behavior in sandpile models designed to resemble experimental granular systems.<ref name="breakdown">[https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.66.051306 Vieira, M. de S. (2002), “Breakdown of self-organized criticality in sandpiles,” ''Physical Review E'' 66, 051306.]</ref>

This reinforces the need to distinguish idealized SOC from actual granular dynamics.

== <math>1/f</math> noise ==

The original SOC papers connected critical dynamics with flicker or <math>1/f</math> noise.

A power spectrum can have approximate form

<math>S(f)\propto f^{-\beta}.</math>

Many other mechanisms can also generate <math>1/f</math>-like spectra.

Spectral slope alone is therefore not evidence of SOC.

== Earthquakes ==

Earthquake distributions and fault models have motivated SOC analogies.

Seismicity shows scale-free features such as Gutenberg–Richter magnitude statistics.

But earthquake physics includes:

* stress transfer;
* friction;
* fault geometry;
* tectonic loading.

A power law in earthquake size does not by itself identify SOC as the mechanism.

== Solar activity ==

Avalanche models have been applied to solar flares and magnetospheric phenomena.

Such applications can be useful when they generate testable predictions.

The physical driving, dissipation, and coupling should be mapped explicitly rather than inferred from burst statistics alone.

== Neural systems ==

Neural avalanches have been interpreted through criticality and SOC frameworks.

Evidence includes scale-free-like event distributions in some preparations.

Competing explanations and sampling effects remain important.

Neural criticality is not synonymous with SOC.

A system can be near a critical point because of regulatory tuning rather than self-organization in the BTW sense.

== Ecology and social systems ==

SOC has also been proposed for ecological, economic, and social processes.

These applications require exceptional caution.

Adaptive agents, institutions, strategic behavior, and nonstationary environments can violate the assumptions of simple driven threshold models.

Metaphorical similarity to avalanches is not enough.

== SOC and self-organization ==

[[Self-organization]] is broader than SOC.

A system can self-organize into:

* patterns;
* oscillations;
* synchronized states;

without being critical.

SOC is a specific hypothesis about self-organization toward a critical-like state.

== SOC and emergence ==

[[Emergence]] is also broader.

All SOC models exhibit emergent system-level statistics.

Not all emergent systems are critical.

The concepts should not be used interchangeably.

== Simulation requirements ==

A credible SOC simulation should report:

* lattice size;
* boundary conditions;
* driving rule;
* toppling rule;
* update order;
* avalanche definition;
* transient removal;
* random seeds.

Results should be repeated across sizes.

Scaling exponents can depend on implementation details.

== Experimental test ==

A strong empirical SOC test asks:

# Is the system driven and dissipative?
# Does it approach a stationary state?
# Is there evidence of scale invariance across increasing system sizes?
# Are temporal and spatial correlations consistent with criticality?
# Does a plausible self-tuning mechanism exist?
# Do alternative models fit equally well?

This is stronger than checking one event histogram.

== Epistemic status ==

SOC is an established theoretical framework with rigorously studied models and important experimental examples.

Its application to any particular real-world system is a separate empirical claim.

The correct stance is therefore:

<pre>
SOC exists as a model class
but a power law does not prove a real system is SOC
</pre>

